{"about":"a0b919ae506615d2d7594201179ce183589fd29077a754b18dafc5214f197797","body":"The conjecture claims we must accept our actions are grounded in 'educated guesswork,' but this phrase itself is a contradiction in terms within a strictly non-inductive framework. An 'educated' guess implies that the education (past experience/tests) improves the quality of the guess. If corroboration adds no probability and implies no truth-likeness that can be relied upon, then the guess is not 'educated' by the tests; it is merely a guess that happens to follow a specific procedural history. Consequently, the preference for better-corroborated theories becomes an arbitrary psychological habit rather than a defensible rational strategy, undermining the claim that Popper offers a logical solution to the problem of action.\n[would settle: A clear distinction between 'psychological confidence' derived from testing and 'rational justification' for action that does not rely on inductive inference.]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}